<graph edgedefault="undirected"><node id="Parenteral Droperidol">
  <data key="d0">Parenteral Droperidol</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Parenteral droperidol (IV or IM) effectiveness for acute migraine relief was equal to or better than prochlorperazine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Haloperidol">
  <data key="d0">Haloperidol</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Haloperidol is a medication administered intravenously, effective for migraine pain relief but associated with high rates of sedation and akathisia.&lt;SEP&gt;Haloperidol (5 mg IV) was effective for migraine pain relief in a trial of 40 hospitalized patients; adverse effects like sedation and akathisia occurred in 80 percent of patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Haloperidol is a butyrophenone antipsychotic studied for the acute treatment of migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Haloperidol 2.5 to 5 mg IV or chlorpromazine 0.1 mg/kg IV might be used in place of prochlorperazine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3968d482efd321ce0e71494d6d8cbde6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Metoclopramide">
  <data key="d0">Metoclopramide</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Metoclopramide is an antiemetic medication and dopamine receptor blocker primarily utilized in emergency and migraine treatment settings. Administered intravenously, it is frequently employed to manage nausea and vomiting, particularly as a common side effect when used alongside dihydroergotamine (DHE) for migraine therapy. Clinical comparisons with other antiemetics, such as haloperidol and prochlorperazine, have shown Metoclopramide to be effective for pain relief and symptom management, though it may be associated with a higher incidence of restlessness compared to some alternatives. Additionally, the drug is used to prevent and treat dystonic reactions, establishing its role as a versatile agent in the acute management of migraine and related gastrointestinal symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Metoclopramide is used to treat undifferentiated headache in the ED.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81fbead48486691cf5945207e3542587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5254cc12db8ae366992b8abc63e9bce0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f74beccf3932fc999c5da6217dd983e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NSAIDs">
  <data key="d0">NSAIDs</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The combined use of a triptan and a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) appears to be more effective than triptan alone.&lt;SEP&gt;NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) are simple analgesics used for mild migraine attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) can be combined with metoclopramide to decrease nausea and vomiting.&lt;SEP&gt;Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), including aspirin, increases the risk of intracranial bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;NSAIDs should be withheld in patients with concern for a hemorrhagic cause of headache or who may require a lumbar puncture.&lt;SEP&gt;NSAIDs should be withheld in patients for whom there remains concern for a hemorrhagic cause of headache or who may require a lumbar puncture (LP).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2878ee960479fec5e0fd7c97d6d44f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b20a73b1bf34e686ee6652ff377b531a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lysine Acetylsalicylate">
  <data key="d0">Lysine Acetylsalicylate</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Oral lysine acetylsalicylate (equivalent to 900 mg of aspirin) plus oral metoclopramide (10 mg) was compared with oral sumatriptan (100 mg) or placebo in a trial of 421 patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Lysine acetylsalicylate is a medication combined with metoclopramide for migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CGRP Antagonists">
  <data key="d0">CGRP Antagonists</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">CGRP antagonists are migraine-specific agents used for acute treatment of migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Pharmacologic modulation of calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) activity appears to mediate trigeminovascular pain transmission in migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;CGRP antagonists are a class of medications used for the acute and preventive treatment of migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;CGRP antagonists are alternative options for migraine treatment, including for patients refractory to triptans.&lt;SEP&gt;CGRP antagonists are medications used for the prevention of cluster headaches, though data on their use is limited and conflicted.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f90692986dfb1f830a27d0b7536f61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2878ee960479fec5e0fd7c97d6d44f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba771d788f7481cea05e87c2e085913d</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711576</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Monoclonal Antibodies">
  <data key="d0">Monoclonal Antibodies</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Monoclonal antibodies directed against the CGRP receptor or ligand are given by periodic injection for migraine prevention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small-Molecule CGRP Antagonists (Gepants)">
  <data key="d0">Small-Molecule CGRP Antagonists (Gepants)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Small-molecule CGRP antagonists (gepants) are oral or intranasal medications with specific formulations effective for acute treatment; others are used for migraine prevention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ubrogepant">
  <data key="d0">Ubrogepant</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Ubrogepant is given as a single oral tablet at either 50 or 100 mg; dose may be repeated in two hours with a maximum daily dose of 200 mg.&lt;SEP&gt;Ubrogepant is a medication used in the acute treatment of migraine, compared against placebo in clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Ubrogepant is a calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) receptor antagonist used for acute migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c43e57d7c9eb41cc91c89593e5618e6</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rimegepant">
  <data key="d0">Rimegepant</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Rimegepant is given at 75 mg as a single oral dose.&lt;SEP&gt;Rimegepant received US FDA approval for acute migraine treatment in February 2020 and is also approved for migraine prevention at a dose of 75 mg once every other day.&lt;SEP&gt;Rimegepant is an oral calcitonin gene-related peptide receptor antagonist used for migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f90692986dfb1f830a27d0b7536f61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c43e57d7c9eb41cc91c89593e5618e6</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FDA Approval Ubrogepant">
  <data key="d0">FDA Approval Ubrogepant</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Ubrogepant received US FDA approval for the treatment of acute migraine in adults in December 2019.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FDA Approval Rimegepant">
  <data key="d0">FDA Approval Rimegepant</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Rimegepant received US FDA approval for acute migraine treatment in February 2020.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rimegepant for Migraine Prevention">
  <data key="d0">Rimegepant for Migraine Prevention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rimegepant is also approved by the US FDA for migraine prevention when given at a dose of 75 mg once every other day.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Placebo">
  <data key="d0">Placebo</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Placebo is an inert substance used as a control in clinical trials to assess the efficacy of active treatments. It serves as a comparator in double-blind, placebo-controlled studies designed to evaluate the effectiveness of migraine treatments, including triptans, ubrogepant, rimegepant, and galcanezumab. In the context of cluster headache, placebo was utilized in randomized trials and double-blind pilot studies for both prophylaxis and acute treatment, such as sphenopalatine ganglion block. Clinical data from these trials often demonstrate a significant difference in outcomes, for example, a 30-minute pain-free rate of 47 percent for active treatments compared to 18 percent for placebo, with participant groups sometimes consisting of 57 and 26 individuals respectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c43e57d7c9eb41cc91c89593e5618e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22a7faa6dc2428acdd3ea3c1593916bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4041840a72080e115133366da601ba0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4bdd16b737b7973c6581c7cc92604b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5254cc12db8ae366992b8abc63e9bce0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3968d482efd321ce0e71494d6d8cbde6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-075c9514b73c9528ed6f8491b449ee40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e949ceaa91563d5020dfddbce0137142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb1965a69687b7da4c479723318f38b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711570</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Akathisia">
  <data key="d0">Akathisia</data>
  <data key="d1">adverseeffect</data>
  <data key="d2">Akathisia is a movement disorder characterized by restlessness and an inability to remain still, which can be induced as a side effect of certain medications.&lt;SEP&gt;Akathisia is a movement disorder characterized by restlessness, occurring as an adverse effect of haloperidol in migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Photophobia">
  <data key="d0">Photophobia</data>
  <data key="d1">migraine-associatedsymptom</data>
  <data key="d2">Photophobia is an increased sensitivity to light, a common symptom of migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Photophobia is a bothersome migraine-associated symptom evaluated in ubrogepant and rimegepant clinical trials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f90692986dfb1f830a27d0b7536f61</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phonophobia">
  <data key="d0">Phonophobia</data>
  <data key="d1">migraine-associatedsymptom</data>
  <data key="d2">Phonophobia is a bothersome migraine-associated symptom evaluated in ubrogepant and rimegepant clinical trials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nausea">
  <data key="d0">Nausea</data>
  <data key="d1">adverseeffect</data>
  <data key="d2">Nausea is a common symptom associated with migraine attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Nausea is a common adverse effect associated with ubrogepant, rimegepant, and other CGRP antagonists.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f90692986dfb1f830a27d0b7536f61</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dizziness">
  <data key="d0">Dizziness</data>
  <data key="d1">adverseeffect</data>
  <data key="d2">Dizziness is a common adverse effect associated with ubrogepant treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f90692986dfb1f830a27d0b7536f61</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dry Mouth">
  <data key="d0">Dry Mouth</data>
  <data key="d1">adverseeffect</data>
  <data key="d2">Dry mouth is a common adverse event associated with ubrogepant treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lower Urinary Tract Infections">
  <data key="d0">Lower Urinary Tract Infections</data>
  <data key="d1">adverseeffect</data>
  <data key="d2">Lower urinary tract infections were common adverse effects associated with rimegepant treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fatigue">
  <data key="d0">Fatigue</data>
  <data key="d1">adverseeffect</data>
  <data key="d2">Fatigue is a common adverse effect associated with both ubrogepant and rimegepant treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Somnolence">
  <data key="d0">Somnolence</data>
  <data key="d1">adverseeffect</data>
  <data key="d2">Somnolence is a common adverse event associated with ubrogepant and rimegepant treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coronary Artery Disease">
  <data key="d0">Coronary Artery Disease</data>
  <data key="d1">contraindication</data>
  <data key="d2">Coronary artery disease is a contraindication for treatment with triptans and/or antiemetics, making CGRP antagonists a suitable alternative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Migraine Prodrome">
  <data key="d0">Migraine Prodrome</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Migraine prodrome refers to the early symptoms (e.g., photophobia, fatigue, neck pain) occurring one to six hours before headache onset.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ubrogepant 50 mg Dose">
  <data key="d0">Ubrogepant 50 mg Dose</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Ubrogepant 50 mg is a dose option for acute migraine treatment, with efficacy data showing 19.2 percent pain freedom at two hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ubrogepant 100 mg Dose">
  <data key="d0">Ubrogepant 100 mg Dose</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Ubrogepant 100 mg is a dose option for acute migraine treatment, with efficacy data showing 21.2 percent pain freedom at two hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rimegepant 75 mg Dose">
  <data key="d0">Rimegepant 75 mg Dose</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Rimegepant 75 mg is a single oral dose for acute migraine treatment, with efficacy data showing 21 percent pain freedom at two hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sumatriptan">
  <data key="d0">Sumatriptan</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Sumatriptan is a triptan medication primarily used for the acute treatment of migraine attacks. It offers diverse routes of administration, including subcutaneous injection, nasal spray, nasal powder, and oral formulations, with subcutaneous delivery providing the fastest onset of action. Clinical studies have compared its efficacy against other treatments, such as lysine acetylsalicylate plus metoclopramide and dihydroergotamine, to assess headache relief and pain-free response.&lt;SEP&gt;Sumatriptan is a preventive agent that can be used safely in conjunction with verapamil.&lt;SEP&gt;Sumatriptan is an effective triptan for acute cluster headache, available via subcutaneous injection (6 mg) or intranasal administration (20 mg).&lt;SEP&gt;Sumatriptan is effective for treating acute cluster headache but was found not effective for preventing attacks in a multicenter placebo-controlled trial.&lt;SEP&gt;Sumatriptan is a method used for preemptive oral treatment during a cluster period.&lt;SEP&gt;Sumatriptan is used to treat cluster headache and was shown to be as effective as prochlorperazine in a trial.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b20a73b1bf34e686ee6652ff377b531a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81fbead48486691cf5945207e3542587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c43e57d7c9eb41cc91c89593e5618e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4041840a72080e115133366da601ba0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f034fb31d8b9231647be0271fbbe11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5254cc12db8ae366992b8abc63e9bce0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27dde5a0572eb3184020966c72e61c89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-288c1ac0daa4f8d9e6711072456bf290&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4bdd16b737b7973c6581c7cc92604b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3968d482efd321ce0e71494d6d8cbde6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-075c9514b73c9528ed6f8491b449ee40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e949ceaa91563d5020dfddbce0137142&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e431a39c5b3ac9ad50817fd6694435f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f74beccf3932fc999c5da6217dd983e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rimegepant Every Other Day">
  <data key="d0">Rimegepant Every Other Day</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Rimegepant is approved for migraine prevention when given at a dose of 75 mg once every other day.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Migraine Attack">
  <data key="d0">Migraine Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A migraine attack is the primary clinical event targeted by acute and preventive migraine treatments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Emergency Department">
  <data key="d0">Emergency Department</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Emergency department is the setting where acute migraine treatments like haloperidol and metoclopramide were compared in a clinical trial.&lt;SEP&gt;The emergency department is a common setting for acute migraine treatment studies and comparisons.&lt;SEP&gt;The Emergency Department is the clinical setting where adults with nontraumatic headache are evaluated.&lt;SEP&gt;Emergency department (ED) is the clinical setting where headache evaluation and imaging decisions are discussed.&lt;SEP&gt;Emergency Department is the clinical setting where initial evaluation and treatment of acute headache occurs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d38115148b4b0478b2ff0c5f850bbcb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d7e2a1fa6fae352f798fe0d6e8e0e62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711750</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triptan">
  <data key="d0">Triptan</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Triptan is a class of medications used for acute migraine therapy, with individual drugs varying in pharmacologic properties, delivery routes, and efficacy profiles.&lt;SEP&gt;Triptans are a drug class used for treating acute migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Triptans are a class of medications used for the acute treatment of migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Triptan is a class of medications (serotonin 5-HT(1B/1D) agonists) used for the acute treatment of migraine attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Triptan is a class of medication that should not be given within 24 hours of Ergotamine or DHE due to risk of arterial vasospasm.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f90692986dfb1f830a27d0b7536f61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f034fb31d8b9231647be0271fbbe11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5254cc12db8ae366992b8abc63e9bce0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f74beccf3932fc999c5da6217dd983e</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Naratriptan">
  <data key="d0">Naratriptan</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Naratriptan is a triptan medication available for oral use only, including pills and orally disintegrating tablets and/or films.&lt;SEP&gt;Naratriptan is a triptan with a slower onset of action and may have the lowest propensity to cause side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Naratriptan is a triptan medication effective and well tolerated in the acute treatment of migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Naratriptan is another triptan medication referenced in the context of predicting treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;Naratriptan is a longer-acting triptan with limited data suggesting benefit for cluster headache prevention.&lt;SEP&gt;Naratriptan is a method used for prophylactic treatment in cluster headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4041840a72080e115133366da601ba0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f034fb31d8b9231647be0271fbbe11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27dde5a0572eb3184020966c72e61c89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-075c9514b73c9528ed6f8491b449ee40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e949ceaa91563d5020dfddbce0137142</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711569</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Almotriptan">
  <data key="d0">Almotriptan</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Almotriptan 12.5 mg was effective for migraine treatment when started early or when headache was moderate to severe.&lt;SEP&gt;Almotriptan is an oral triptan noted for having a high likelihood of consistent success in clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Almotriptan is one of the available triptan preparations for acute migraine treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Almotriptan is a triptan medication studied in trials comparing early versus delayed treatment of migraine attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b20a73b1bf34e686ee6652ff377b531a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4041840a72080e115133366da601ba0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f034fb31d8b9231647be0271fbbe11ff</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zolmitriptan">
  <data key="d0">Zolmitriptan</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Zolmitriptan is an acute migraine therapy proven effective in randomized placebo-controlled trials and meta-analysis, with a dose-response relationship where 2.5 mg is often the optimal starting dose.&lt;SEP&gt;Zolmitriptan is a triptan medication available for both nasal and oral use.&lt;SEP&gt;Zolmitriptan is a triptan medication used for acute migraine attacks in adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Zolmitriptan is a triptan medication studied for benefits in treating highly disabled patients while pain is mild.&lt;SEP&gt;Zolmitriptan is another beneficial triptan treatment option for acute cluster headache, available via intranasal administration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4041840a72080e115133366da601ba0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f034fb31d8b9231647be0271fbbe11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27dde5a0572eb3184020966c72e61c89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711569</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Double-Blind Study">
  <data key="d0">Double-Blind Study</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Double-blind study is a research methodology where neither the participants nor the researchers know who receives the treatment or placebo.&lt;SEP&gt;Double-Blind Study is a study design where neither the participants nor the researchers know who receives the treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Double-blind study is a study design where neither the participants nor the researchers know the treatment assignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c43e57d7c9eb41cc91c89593e5618e6</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multicenter Study">
  <data key="d0">Multicenter Study</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Multicenter study involves multiple clinical sites collaborating in the research process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dose Range-Finding Study">
  <data key="d0">Dose Range-Finding Study</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dose range-finding study is a type of clinical trial designed to determine the appropriate dose range of a medication.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Serotonin Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Serotonin Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Concerns have been raised about serotonin syndrome in patients using triptans in combination with SSRIs or SNRIs, though the risk appears very low.&lt;SEP&gt;Serotonin syndrome refers to symptoms that require monitoring when combining certain medications.&lt;SEP&gt;Serotonin syndrome is a potential adverse risk associated with the combination of triptans and certain antidepressants (SSRI/SNRI).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b20a73b1bf34e686ee6652ff377b531a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5254cc12db8ae366992b8abc63e9bce0</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711452</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cutaneous Allodynia">
  <data key="d0">Cutaneous Allodynia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cutaneous allodynia is the perception of pain produced by innocuous stimulation of normal skin, making triptans less effective once established.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutaneous allodynia is a condition where normally non-painful stimuli cause pain, which can develop during migraine attacks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b20a73b1bf34e686ee6652ff377b531a</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Myocardial Infarction">
  <data key="d0">Myocardial Infarction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Myocardial infarction (heart attack) is a cardiovascular risk associated with triptan use, particularly in certain patient populations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stroke">
  <data key="d0">Stroke</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Stroke is a cardiovascular risk associated with triptan use, particularly in patients with existing risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Stroke is a serious cause of headache associated with focal neurologic deficits.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oral Sumatriptan">
  <data key="d0">Oral Sumatriptan</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Oral sumatriptan is a route of administration for the medication, studied for its efficacy and response predictors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triptan-Naproxen Combination">
  <data key="d0">Triptan-Naproxen Combination</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The combination of sumatriptan and naproxen is a treatment approach for acute migraine attacks in adults.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
