  <data key="d0">Triptan-Sumatriptan/Naproxen</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Sumatriptan-naproxen is a randomized trial treatment for acute migraine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triptan-SSRI/SNRI Interaction">
  <data key="d0">Triptan-SSRI/SNRI Interaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The combination of triptans with SSRIs or SNRIs carries a risk of serotonin syndrome, as evaluated in multiple studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triptan-Ergotamine Interaction">
  <data key="d0">Triptan-Ergotamine Interaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adverse cardiovascular events are associated with the combination of triptans and ergotamines for migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Triptan Cardiovascular Safety">
  <data key="d0">Triptan Cardiovascular Safety</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cardiovascular tolerability and safety of triptans are reviewed in clinical data, focusing on stroke and myocardial infarction risks.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Migraine Treatment Guidelines">
  <data key="d0">Migraine Treatment Guidelines</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Migraine treatment approach is influenced by attack severity, associated nausea/vomiting, site of care, and patient-specific factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical evidence and meta-analyses inform the comparative efficacy and safety of triptans for migraine abortive treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22a7faa6dc2428acdd3ea3c1593916bb</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clinical Trial Meta-Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Clinical Trial Meta-Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Multiple treatment comparison meta-analysis is a statistical method used to compare the efficacy of different triptans.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="5-HT(1B/1D) Receptor">
  <data key="d0">5-HT(1B/1D) Receptor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The 5-HT(1B/1D) receptor is the biological target for the serotonin 5-HT(1B/1D) agonists (triptans) used in migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nexus">
  <data key="d0">Nexus</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nexus refers to the connection or association between triptan use and serotonin syndrome risk when combined with antidepressants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trial Identifier">
  <data key="d0">Trial Identifier</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Trial identifiers (e.g., numbers 71-94) are references to specific clinical studies and publications in the field of migraine research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Antiemetics">
  <data key="d0">Antiemetics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Antiemetics are used to treat nausea and vomiting associated with migraine attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Antiemetics are medications used to prevent or treat nausea and vomiting associated with migraine attacks.&lt;SEP&gt;Antiemetics are medications used to treat nausea and vomiting in acute migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Antiemetics are medications used to prevent or treat nausea and vomiting, often added when attacks involve severe nausea.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2878ee960479fec5e0fd7c97d6d44f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5254cc12db8ae366992b8abc63e9bce0</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ergots">
  <data key="d0">Ergots</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Ergots are a class of medications used for migraine treatment, including parenteral dihydroergotamine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CGRP antagonists">
  <data key="d0">CGRP antagonists</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">CGRP antagonists are a class of medications used for migraine treatment, including nasal formulations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lasmiditan">
  <data key="d0">Lasmiditan</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Lasmiditan is a migraine-specific agent used for acute treatment of migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Lasmiditan is a medication used for migraine treatment, specifically mentioned as a nonoral migraine-specific option.&lt;SEP&gt;Lasmiditan is a selective serotonin 1F receptor agonist effective for the acute treatment of migraine and lacks vasoconstrictor activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Lasmiditan is a medication alternative for migraine treatment, particularly for patients who cannot use triptans.&lt;SEP&gt;Lasmiditan is a medication used for the acute treatment of migraine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f90692986dfb1f830a27d0b7536f61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2878ee960479fec5e0fd7c97d6d44f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c43e57d7c9eb41cc91c89593e5618e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Status migrainosus">
  <data key="d0">Status migrainosus</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Status migrainosus is defined as a debilitating migraine attack lasting for more than 72 hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Variable attacks">
  <data key="d0">Variable attacks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Variable attacks refer to migraine attacks that vary in severity, time of onset, and association with vomiting and nausea.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Emergency settings">
  <data key="d0">Emergency settings</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Emergency settings refer to the treatment of migraine attacks in emergency departments or urgent care settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ketorolac">
  <data key="d0">Ketorolac</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Parenteral ketorolac (30 mg IV or 60 mg IM) was found effective for acute migraine in comparison with other agents.&lt;SEP&gt;Ketorolac is a parenteral medication used in the treatment of status migrainosus.&lt;SEP&gt;Ketorolac was reviewed systematically for its use in the treatment of acute migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Ketorolac is a parenteral medication suggested for combination treatment in status migrainosus.&lt;SEP&gt;In the United States, this treatment would probably be ketorolac 15 to 30 mg intravenously (IV) and prochlorperazine 10 mg.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4bdd16b737b7973c6581c7cc92604b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dopamine receptor blocker">
  <data key="d0">Dopamine receptor blocker</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dopamine receptor blocker is a method used in combination with intravenous fluids for status migrainosus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711453</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prochlorperazine">
  <data key="d0">Prochlorperazine</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Prochlorperazine is an antiemetic-dopamine receptor blocker used in emergency settings, available in intravenous or intramuscular forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Prochlorperazine is an intravenous or intramuscular antiemetic used for acute migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Prochlorperazine is a dopamine receptor blocker used to prevent dystonic reactions and manage symptoms in status migrainosus.&lt;SEP&gt;Prochlorperazine is an antiemetic medication compared with metoclopramide for acute migraine treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Prochlorperazine was shown to be as effective as subcutaneous sumatriptan in a small randomized trial.&lt;SEP&gt;In the United States, this treatment would probably be ketorolac 15 to 30 mg intravenously (IV) and prochlorperazine 10 mg.&lt;SEP&gt;In a small randomized trial, prochlorperazine was shown to be as effective as subcutaneous sumatriptan.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5254cc12db8ae366992b8abc63e9bce0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chlorpromazine">
  <data key="d0">Chlorpromazine</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Chlorpromidine is an antiemetic used in emergency settings, administered as a slow intravenous infusion.&lt;SEP&gt;Chlorpromazine is a medication used for acute migraine treatment, noted for providing adequate pain relief but associated with higher adverse effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Chlorpromazine is a dopamine receptor blocker used in the management of status migrainosus.&lt;SEP&gt;Chlorpromazine is an intravenous medication used in the emergency department treatment of migraines.&lt;SEP&gt;Chlorpromazine is an antiemetic medication commonly administered with DHE to counteract nausea.&lt;SEP&gt;Chlorpromazine is an antiemetic medication that can be administered with dihydroergotamine (DHE) to manage nausea.&lt;SEP&gt;Haloperidol 2.5 to 5 mg IV or chlorpromazine 0.1 mg/kg IV might be used in place of prochlorperazine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3968d482efd321ce0e71494d6d8cbde6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f74beccf3932fc999c5da6217dd983e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dihydroergotamine">
  <data key="d0">Dihydroergotamine</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Dihydroergotamine is a medication primarily used for the acute treatment of migraine and cluster headaches. As an alpha-adrenergic agonist and a potent 5-HT&lt;sub&gt;1b/1d&lt;/sub&gt; receptor agonist, it functions by constricting blood vessels and inhibiting the release of inflammatory substances in the brain. It is available in multiple formulations for administration, including subcutaneous, intranasal, and intravenous routes, and is frequently combined with antiemetic drugs to manage nausea, a common side effect. While primarily indicated for migraine, it also serves as an alternative therapy for cluster headache attacks in patients who do not respond to or tolerate initial treatments. Additionally, it may be used as adjunctive therapy during the early stages of verapamil administration. Administration routes are typically intravenous or intramuscular for acute settings.&lt;SEP&gt;Dihydroergotamine 1 mg IV may be effective for headaches unresponsive to NSAID and dopamine antagonist combination, especially those with migrainous features like photophobia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f90692986dfb1f830a27d0b7536f61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2878ee960479fec5e0fd7c97d6d44f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81fbead48486691cf5945207e3542587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c43e57d7c9eb41cc91c89593e5618e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3bc3f7fc9283c8a89abea7bd6a2953&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697366b5ec6fccd401f60e32f476bcb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sodium valproate">
  <data key="d0">Sodium valproate</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Sodium valproate is a parenteral medication that may be warranted depending on response to initial therapy for status migrainosus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711454</data>
