</node>
<node id="Migraine">
  <data key="d0">Migraine</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Migraine is a neurological condition characterized by recurrent, moderate to severe headaches, often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and heightened sensitivity to light (photophobia) and sound (phonophobia). It presents as acute attacks that can be debilitating, frequently associated with symptoms such as nausea and sensitivity to environmental stimuli. While primarily defined by headache, the condition encompasses a spectrum of subtypes, including those with aura or brainstem involvement. Acute management typically involves triptan therapy, with alternative options such as dihydroergotamine and ergotamine utilized in refractory cases. The disorder is a focal point of ongoing clinical trials and pharmacological research aimed at developing novel treatments and improving patient outcomes. It is a primary headache syndrome frequently associated with cluster headache and is studied in the context of treatments such as fremanezumab. Migraine can present with visual aura and is distinguished by positive phenomena; it is considered a benign primary headache disorder. Diagnosis typically requires at least four prior episodes. Furthermore, the majority of patients with these headache types will ultimately be diagnosed with either migraine or cluster headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df12cd945c38984efea15b2addfde26f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f90692986dfb1f830a27d0b7536f61&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2878ee960479fec5e0fd7c97d6d44f85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca64ee464064b0ec87b1acd587b7d970&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b20a73b1bf34e686ee6652ff377b531a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81fbead48486691cf5945207e3542587&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c43e57d7c9eb41cc91c89593e5618e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-288c1ac0daa4f8d9e6711072456bf290&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74803e1d9bd621760a74773f08649443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22a7faa6dc2428acdd3ea3c1593916bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4041840a72080e115133366da601ba0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f034fb31d8b9231647be0271fbbe11ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4bdd16b737b7973c6581c7cc92604b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3968d482efd321ce0e71494d6d8cbde6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27dde5a0572eb3184020966c72e61c89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb1965a69687b7da4c479723318f38b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-469ba9f85eff0d1ef2fc5b0807c28e01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d7e2a1fa6fae352f798fe0d6e8e0e62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Emergency_evaluation_of_the_adult_with_new_nontraumatic.PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Diphenhydramine">
  <data key="d0">Diphenhydramine</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Diphenhydramine is used adjunctively with parenteral antiemetics to prevent akathisia and dystonic reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Diphenhydramine is used to prevent akathisia and acute dystonic reactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Diphenhydramine is an adjunctive medication used to prevent dystonic reactions when metoclopramide or prochlorperazine are administered.&lt;SEP&gt;Pretreatment with 12.5 mg of diphenhydramine or 1 mg of benztropine is suggested to avoid akathisia or other extrapyramidal symptoms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cfaea66e0e1b59fcf11083fdc86fa505&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eced74bbae68c4d04d0938805d606b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5254cc12db8ae366992b8abc63e9bce0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
