<node id="INP104">
  <data key="d0">INP104</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">INP104 is a Precision Olfactory Delivery (POD®) of dihydroergotamine mesylate used in a Phase 3 study for acute migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-27096fcb7fa30c9fea180ea7b54f322c</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711458</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="POD®">
  <data key="d0">POD®</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">POD® (Precision Olfactory Delivery) is a delivery method for dihydroergotamine mesylate used in acute migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-27096fcb7fa30c9fea180ea7b54f322c</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711458</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dihydroergotamine mesylate">
  <data key="d0">Dihydroergotamine mesylate</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Dihydroergotamine mesylate is the active pharmaceutical ingredient delivered via INP104/POD® for migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-27096fcb7fa30c9fea180ea7b54f322c</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711458</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Opioid Medications">
  <data key="d0">Opioid Medications</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Opioid medications are discussed as first-line therapy for severe migraine or recommended against for acute migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711458</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pregnancy">
  <data key="d0">Pregnancy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The treatment of migraine in pregnant patients differs due to concerns about adverse fetal drug effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f74beccf3932fc999c5da6217dd983e</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Medication Overuse Headache (MOH)">
  <data key="d0">Medication Overuse Headache (MOH)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Medication overuse headache (MOH), also called analgesic rebound headache, is a common disorder with significant morbidity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simple Analgesics">
  <data key="d0">Simple Analgesics</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Some patients with migraine have an optimal response with simple analgesics, including aspirin, NSAIDs, and acetaminophen.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2878ee960479fec5e0fd7c97d6d44f85</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)">
  <data key="d0">Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Drugs (NSAIDs)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">NSAIDs with reported efficacy in migraine therapy include aspirin, ibuprofen, naproxen sodium, diclofenac, diclofenac epolamine, tolfenamic acid, celecoxib, and dexketoprofen.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Indomethacin">
  <data key="d0">Indomethacin</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Indomethacin is a potent NSAID available in suppository form, which may be helpful for nauseated patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Indomethacin is considered ineffective for cluster headache in the short term, but there are reports of occasional patients meeting diagnostic criteria who responded to indomethacin when used for several weeks at higher doses (≥225 mg daily).&lt;SEP&gt;Indomethacin is a method used in cluster headache, particularly responsive in certain case reports.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-075c9514b73c9528ed6f8491b449ee40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e949ceaa91563d5020dfddbce0137142</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711569</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Population-based Randomized Placebo-controlled Trial">
  <data key="d0">Population-based Randomized Placebo-controlled Trial</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A population-based randomized placebo-controlled trial of 289 patients with self-reported migraine found acetaminophen 1000 mg to be highly effective.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Serotonin 1b/1d Agonists">
  <data key="d0">Serotonin 1b/1d Agonists</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Triptans are serotonin 1b/1d agonists effective for acute migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aspirin-Acetaminophen-Caffeine">
  <data key="d0">Aspirin-Acetaminophen-Caffeine</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The combination of acetaminophen-aspirin-caffeine was found to alleviate headaches in patients with uncomplicated migraine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parenteral Ketorolac">
  <data key="d0">Parenteral Ketorolac</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Parenteral ketorolac (30 mg intravenous [IV] or 60 mg intramuscular [IM]) was effective for acute migraine in comparison with other agents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Oral Solution of Powdered Diclofenac Potassium">
  <data key="d0">Oral Solution of Powdered Diclofenac Potassium</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An oral solution of powdered diclofenac potassium (50 mg) mixed with water produced more rapid and effective pain relief at two hours than tablets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9781a20ce9291545e13c64cc40854f23</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Central Nervous System">
  <data key="d0">Central Nervous System</data>
  <data key="d1">bodysystem</data>
  <data key="d2">The central or peripheral nervous system can be stimulated with electrical current or magnetic field.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ACME Trial">
  <data key="d0">ACME Trial</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The ACME trial randomly assigned subjects with acute migraine to treatment with external trigeminal nerve stimulation or sham stimulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Visual Analog Scale">
  <data key="d0">Visual Analog Scale</data>
  <data key="d1">measurement</data>
  <data key="d2">Pain was assessed using a visual analog scale ranging from 0 to 10.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="External Trigeminal Nerve Stimulator Device">
  <data key="d0">External Trigeminal Nerve Stimulator Device</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The external device delivering combined occipital and trigeminal nerve stimulation is approved for marketing in the United States, Canada, Europe, and several additional countries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Remote Electrical Neuromodulation">
  <data key="d0">Remote Electrical Neuromodulation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A device applying nonpainful electrical skin stimulation can reduce acute migraine pain.&lt;SEP&gt;Remote Electrical Neuromodulation is a therapeutic method that relieves acute migraine through randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled multicenter trials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74803e1d9bd621760a74773f08649443</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Smartphone-Controlled Armband">
  <data key="d0">Smartphone-Controlled Armband</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The stimulating device consists of an armband with rubber electrodes and a power source, controlled with a smartphone.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sham Stimulation">
  <data key="d0">Sham Stimulation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Sham stimulation was designed to produce a sensation similar to active stimulation but with a frequency too low to induce pain inhibition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (TMS)">
  <data key="d0">Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (TMS)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The efficacy of single-pulse transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) was demonstrated in a sham-controlled trial.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Portable TMS Device">
  <data key="d0">Portable TMS Device</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The portable TMS device is available in the United Kingdom and the United States.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Noninvasive Vagus Nerve Stimulation (nVNS)">
  <data key="d0">Noninvasive Vagus Nerve Stimulation (nVNS)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Noninvasive vagus nerve stimulation (nVNS) may be beneficial for the acute treatment of episodic migraine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PRESTO Trial">
  <data key="d0">PRESTO Trial</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The PRESTO trial randomly assigned over 240 participants to treatment with a proprietary nVNS device or a sham device.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Peripheral Nerve Blocks">
  <data key="d0">Peripheral Nerve Blocks</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Nerve blocks targeting the occipital nerve, sphenopalatine ganglion, and trigeminal nerve branches are options for the acute treatment of migraine.&lt;SEP&gt;Peripheral Nerve Blocks are expert consensus recommendations for performance in narrative review for headaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74803e1d9bd621760a74773f08649443</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Occipital Nerve">
  <data key="d0">Occipital Nerve</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The occipital nerve is a target for peripheral nerve blocks in migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sphenopalatine Ganglion">
  <data key="d0">Sphenopalatine Ganglion</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">The sphenopalatine ganglion is a target for peripheral nerve blocks in migraine treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Sphenopalatine ganglion is a nerve structure targeted for migraine treatment via intranasal devices.&lt;SEP&gt;The sphenopalatine ganglion is an autonomic pathway targeted in surgical procedures for cluster headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22a7faa6dc2428acdd3ea3c1593916bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fcccbfac9c2ef745a159d63b88ddc90</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711572</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trigeminal Nerve Branches">
  <data key="d0">Trigeminal Nerve Branches</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Trigeminal nerve branches (eg, supraorbital, auriculo-temporal) are targets for peripheral nerve blocks in migraine treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Trigeminal nerve branches include supraorbital and auriculo-temporal, utilized in migraine treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22a7faa6dc2428acdd3ea3c1593916bb</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Episodic Migraine">
  <data key="d0">Episodic Migraine</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Episodic migraine is a condition tested in trials of remote electrical neuromodulation and transcranial magnetic stimulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711459</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Seizure">
  <data key="d0">Seizure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Patients who have epilepsy should not use TMS due to theoretical concern of triggering seizures.&lt;SEP&gt;Seizure is a common presentation in cerebral vein thrombosis, brain abscess, and hypertensive encephalopathy.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c97fa32b2d7b605eb00ea730f0821e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46a91a6ca3bd33e40fa248d0f9db07e9</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Acute treatment of migraine in adults(1).PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Cannot_miss_diagnoses_in_the_ED_patient_with_acute.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mazaheri S">
  <data key="d0">Mazaheri S</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mazaheri S is a researcher who conducted a clinical trial comparing intravenous sodium valproate and dexamethasone for acute migraine headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-74803e1d9bd621760a74773f08649443</data>
