  <data key="d0">Louter MA</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Louter MA is an author of a study on cluster headache and depression.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7f561f3e1980fae1e2fbac5ed5564517</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Greater occipital nerve blocks">
  <data key="d0">Greater occipital nerve blocks</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Alternative medications such as greater occipital nerve blocks for some patients who do not respond to initial preventive therapies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-697366b5ec6fccd401f60e32f476bcb8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECG">
  <data key="d0">ECG</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">ECG is used to screen for baseline cardiac arrhythmia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e431a39c5b3ac9ad50817fd6694435f0</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CGRP">
  <data key="d0">CGRP</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">CGRP (calcitonin gene-related peptide) is the ligand that galcanezumab binds to.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e431a39c5b3ac9ad50817fd6694435f0</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DHE">
  <data key="d0">DHE</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">DHE is an intravenous medication administered as a 1 mg bolus, repeatable at one hour with a maximum dose of 3 mg in 24 hours and a general weekly limit of 6 mg, sometimes exceeded in inpatients receiving repetitive protocols.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f74beccf3932fc999c5da6217dd983e</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Verapamil dosing">
  <data key="d0">Verapamil dosing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Verapamil may be given alone or with a short course of glucocorticoids to help provide rapid suppression of cluster headache attacks during the initial titration; starting dose is 80 mg three times daily, increasing total daily dose by 120 mg every 14 days as tolerated; regular-release formulation requires three divided doses daily, while sustained-release requires two divided doses daily; most patients respond to a total daily dose of 240 to 480 mg, with some requiring up to 960 mg for full benefit; an adequate trial entails use of a total daily dose of 480 to 960 mg before the medication is regarded as a failure; benefit is usually seen within two to three weeks; when the bout is ended, verapamil must be gradually reduced over two to four weeks and finally stopped.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f74beccf3932fc999c5da6217dd983e</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Glucocorticoid coadministration">
  <data key="d0">Glucocorticoid coadministration</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">For most patients with cluster headaches who tolerate glucocorticoids, a short course of oral prednisone is typically given during the initial titration of verapamil; preferred regimen is prednisone 100 mg once a day for three days, then tapering by decreasing the dose 10 mg every third day; dose and tapering regimen may be individualized according to cluster episode patterns; efficacy was reported in a short-term treatment trial where patients starting verapamil at 40 mg three times daily and increasing every three days up to a total daily dose of 360 mg had fewer cluster attacks in the first week than those assigned to placebo (difference -2.4 attacks; 95% CI -4.8 to -0.03); adverse effects were mild and similar in both groups; high-dose verapamil is associated with increased incidence of ECG abnormalities including heart block and bradycardia; therefore, an ECG should be obtained after each dose increment above a total daily dose of 480 mg to assess for cardiac conduction delays; some experts recommend a pretreatment ECG to screen for baseline cardiac arrhythmia; other side effects of verapamil include edema, gastrointestinal discomfort, constipation, dull headache, and gingival hyperplasia; verapamil is usually well tolerated and can be used safely with sumatriptan, ergotamine, glucocorticoids, and other preventive agents; efficacy for prevention in cluster headache comes from observational experience and some trial data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f74beccf3932fc999c5da6217dd983e</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ECG monitoring requirement">
  <data key="d0">ECG monitoring requirement</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An ECG should be obtained after each dose increment above a total daily dose of 480 mg to assess for cardiac conduction delays; some experts recommend getting a pretreatment ECG to screen for baseline cardiac arrhythmia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2f74beccf3932fc999c5da6217dd983e</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nighttime attacks">
  <data key="d0">Nighttime attacks</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305</data>
  <data key="d2">Oral ergotamine can be used as preventive treatment (evening dosage) for attacks that occur only at night.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711577</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prophylactic medication tapering">
  <data key="d0">Prophylactic medication tapering</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305</data>
  <data key="d2">For patients with episodic cluster headache, prophylactic medications should be tapered after the expected duration of the cluster has passed.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711578</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dose reduction trial">
  <data key="d0">Dose reduction trial</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305</data>
  <data key="d2">For patients with chronic cluster headache who have a good response to preventive pharmacotherapy, a dose reduction trial of preventive medications every three months is suggested.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711578</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Subcutaneous sumatriptan">
  <data key="d0">Subcutaneous sumatriptan</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305</data>
  <data key="d2">Subcutaneous sumatriptan (6 mg) is beneficial for acute cluster headache, achieving pain-free status in about 75 percent of patients within 20 minutes.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711578</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Medication restart">
  <data key="d0">Medication restart</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305</data>
  <data key="d2">The drugs can be restarted at the lowest effective dose if symptoms recur.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711578</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intranasal sumatriptan">
  <data key="d0">Intranasal sumatriptan</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305</data>
  <data key="d2">Intranasal sumatriptan (20 mg) is effective for acute cluster headache with a higher response rate at 30 minutes compared to placebo.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711578</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intranasal zolmitriptan">
  <data key="d0">Intranasal zolmitriptan</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9064f1ec2a51a8fb5fc12ef54afba305</data>
  <data key="d2">Intranasal zolmitriptan is another beneficial treatment option for acute cluster headache.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711578</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Global Tech Index">
  <data key="d0">Global Tech Index</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a3bc3f7fc9283c8a89abea7bd6a2953&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-feb1965a69687b7da4c479723318f38b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697366b5ec6fccd401f60e32f476bcb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d2">The decline in the Global Tech Index is part of the broader market selloff driven by investor concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;The Global Tech Index tracks the performance of major technology stocks and experienced a 3.4% decline today.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF&lt;SEP&gt;iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injection Site Rotation">
  <data key="d0">Injection Site Rotation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3</data>
  <data key="d2">The injection site should be rotated to reduce the risk of local reactions with subcutaneous administration.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711580</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Antiemetic Medication">
  <data key="d0">Antiemetic Medication</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3</data>
  <data key="d2">Intravenous DHE is typically administered with an antiemetic medication due to nausea being a common side effect.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711580</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cardiovascular Risks">
  <data key="d0">Cardiovascular Risks</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3</data>
  <data key="d2">Triptans should be avoided in patients with ischemic cardiovascular disease or stroke, Prinzmetal angina, or uncontrolled arterial hypertension.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711580</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intravenous DHE">
  <data key="d0">Intravenous DHE</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3</data>
  <data key="d2">Intravenous DHE is typically administered with an antiemetic medication due to nausea being a common side effect.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711581</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Alternative Therapies">
  <data key="d0">Alternative Therapies</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3</data>
  <data key="d2">For patients who do not tolerate or respond to initial therapies, lidocaine is suggested as an alternative agent.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711581</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pain Side Administration">
  <data key="d0">Pain Side Administration</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3</data>
  <data key="d2">Intranasal triptans are administered contralateral to the pain side because patients with cluster headache often have rhinorrhea or nasal congestion ipsilateral to the pain.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711581</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cardiovascular Contraindications">
  <data key="d0">Cardiovascular Contraindications</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3</data>
  <data key="d2">Triptans should be avoided in patients with ischemic cardiovascular disease or stroke, Prinzmetal angina, or uncontrolled arterial hypertension.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711581</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nasal Spray Preparation">
  <data key="d0">Nasal Spray Preparation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f8aa2f95b44d688508b19c8d0fcbc4f3</data>
  <data key="d2">Lidocaine nasal sprays are not commercially available in the United States but may be prepared by a compounding pharmacy.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711581</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Neurostimulator Lead Migration">
  <data key="d0">Neurostimulator Lead Migration</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8fcccbfac9c2ef745a159d63b88ddc90</data>
  <data key="d2">Neurostimulator lead migration is a serious adverse event involving device displacement.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711581</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Device Implantation">
  <data key="d0">Device Implantation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8fcccbfac9c2ef745a159d63b88ddc90</data>
  <data key="d2">Long-term safety of device implantation requires additional data to establish optimal stimulation protocols.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711581</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Serious Adverse Events">
  <data key="d0">Serious Adverse Events</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8fcccbfac9c2ef745a159d63b88ddc90</data>
  <data key="d2">Neurostimulator lead migration is a serious adverse event involving device displacement.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711582</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Surgical Procedures">
  <data key="d0">Surgical Procedures</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8fcccbfac9c2ef745a159d63b88ddc90</data>
  <data key="d2">Destructive surgical procedures carry risks including trigeminal neuralgia, anesthesia dolorosa, hypesthesia, meningitis, and cerebrospinal fluid leak.</data>
  <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD - Uptodate - Cluster headache_ Treatment and prognosis.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711582</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
