<node id="Intraocular hemorrhage">
  <data key="d0">Intraocular hemorrhage</data>
  <data key="d1">finding</data>
  <data key="d2">Retinal or subhyaloid hemorrhage can result from trauma or spontaneous SAH.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Visual changes or loss">
  <data key="d0">Visual changes or loss</data>
  <data key="d1">finding</data>
  <data key="d2">Decline or loss of vision can occur from vascular compromise in giant cell arteritis or carotid artery dissection, or as a result of increased intraocular pressure in acute angle-closure glaucoma.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Field cuts">
  <data key="d0">Field cuts</data>
  <data key="d1">finding</data>
  <data key="d2">Field cuts can be seen with any process that involves the optic nerve, chiasm, optic radiations, or occipital cortex.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pupillary asymmetry">
  <data key="d0">Pupillary asymmetry</data>
  <data key="d1">finding</data>
  <data key="d2">An abnormally small pupil associated with other findings of Horner syndrome suggests a carotid dissection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unilateral large poorly reactive pupil">
  <data key="d0">Unilateral large poorly reactive pupil</data>
  <data key="d1">finding</data>
  <data key="d2">A unilateral, large, and poorly reactive pupil may suggest a lesion that compresses the oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III) such as an expanding aneurysm.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abnormal extraocular movements">
  <data key="d0">Abnormal extraocular movements</data>
  <data key="d1">finding</data>
  <data key="d2">Dysfunction in extraocular muscles or associated cranial nerves (III, IV, VI) leads to diplopia and may indicate an enlarging aneurysm, elevated ICP from hydrocephalus, or cavernous sinus neoplasm.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abnormalities of the temporal artery">
  <data key="d0">Abnormalities of the temporal artery</data>
  <data key="d1">finding</data>
  <data key="d2">Abnormalities of the temporal artery (eg, diminished pulse, swelling, or tenderness) are highly suggestive of giant cell arteritis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carotid bruit">
  <data key="d0">Carotid bruit</data>
  <data key="d1">finding</data>
  <data key="d2">A carotid bruit may accompany carotid artery dissection but is more commonly due to atherosclerotic plaque.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nasal discharge">
  <data key="d0">Nasal discharge</data>
  <data key="d1">finding</data>
  <data key="d2">Nasal discharge associated with sinus tenderness or signs of dental infection may reflect the cause of headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CHOICE OF IMAGING EXAM">
  <data key="d0">CHOICE OF IMAGING EXAM</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CT or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the head is the preferred examination if the headache requires imaging evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CTA or MRA examinations">
  <data key="d0">CTA or MRA examinations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CTA or MRA examinations image the arteries, veins, or both, depending on the indication, usually requiring IV contrast administration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Examinations tailored for imaging the orbits">
  <data key="d0">Examinations tailored for imaging the orbits</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Examinations tailored for imaging the orbits, cavernous sinus, and ear (encompassing the skull base and pituitary), face and maxilla (encompassing the paranasal sinuses), or the temporomandibular joint are sometimes added to the head imaging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Effective radiation dose">
  <data key="d0">Effective radiation dose</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Approximate effective radiation dose is 2 mSv for a head CT and 4 to 5 mSv for head CTA.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ACR Appropriateness Criteria">
  <data key="d0">ACR Appropriateness Criteria</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The ACR Appropriateness Criteria provides general guidance for many common clinical scenarios of headache.&lt;SEP&gt;ACR Appropriateness Criteria are guidelines for appropriate imaging utilization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Patients without high-risk features">
  <data key="d0">Patients without high-risk features</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Patients with a history of prior headaches who present to the emergency department.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="American College of Emergency Physicians">
  <data key="d0">American College of Emergency Physicians</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The American College of Emergency Physicians is a professional organization that provides clinical policy statements on acute headache management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horner Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Horner Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d1">creature</data>
  <data key="d2">Horner syndrome is a neurological condition characterized by miosis, ptosis, and anhidrosis, often associated with carotid or vertebral artery dissection.&lt;SEP&gt;Horner syndrome is a clinical clue that may indicate arterial dissection in the absence of thunderclap onset.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intracranial Mass Lesion">
  <data key="d0">Intracranial Mass Lesion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intracranial mass lesion refers to primary or metastatic neoplasm, abscess, or hematoma that can cause increased intracranial pressure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carotid Artery Dissection">
  <data key="d0">Carotid Artery Dissection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Carotid artery dissection is a tear in the carotid artery, raised as a possibility by headache with exertion.&lt;SEP&gt;Carotid artery dissection is a vascular condition where headache may radiate into the neck and a significant percentage of patients manifest Horner syndrome, with nearly 60 percent of patients having brain ischemia or infarction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertebral Artery Dissection">
  <data key="d0">Vertebral Artery Dissection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertebral artery dissection is a vascular condition associated with Horner syndrome in a smaller percentage of cases compared to carotid dissection, and nearly 10 percent of patients present with isolated headache or neck pain.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Increased Intracranial Pressure (ICP)">
  <data key="d0">Increased Intracranial Pressure (ICP)</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Increased intracranial pressure is a clinical concern suspected when headache is accompanied by papilledema, focal neurologic deficit, or repeated nausea and vomiting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lumbar Puncture (LP)">
  <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture (LP)</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Lumbar puncture is a procedure performed to measure opening pressure and evaluate for underlying infection, often following brain imaging in suspected idiopathic intracranial hypertension.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711752</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Head CT">
  <data key="d0">Head CT</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Head CT is an imaging modality used to evaluate for findings associated with elevated ICP, such as hydrocephalus, hemorrhage, and mass effect, prior to lumbar puncture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Head MRI">
  <data key="d0">Head MRI</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Head MRI without and with contrast is used to evaluate for intracranial mass lesions, hydrocephalus, or cerebral edema.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carboxyhemoglobin Concentration">
  <data key="d0">Carboxyhemoglobin Concentration</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Carboxyhemoglobin concentration is a diagnostic data point measured with cooximetry using an arterial blood gas sample to confirm carbon monoxide poisoning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intraocular Pressure">
  <data key="d0">Intraocular Pressure</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Elevated intraocular pressure confirms the diagnosis of acute angle-closure glaucoma.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Venous Pulsations">
  <data key="d0">Venous Pulsations</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Venous pulsations observed on fundoscopy are strong evidence of normal intracranial pressure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Arterial Dissection">
  <data key="d0">Arterial Dissection</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Arterial dissection is a medical condition involving tearing of the arterial wall, commonly presenting with headache or neck pain.&lt;SEP&gt;Arterial dissection is a less common cause of headache in the pregnant or postpartum patient.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Brain Ischemia or Infarction">
  <data key="d0">Brain Ischemia or Infarction</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Brain ischemia or infarction occurs in nearly 60 percent of patients with arterial dissection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trauma">
  <data key="d0">Trauma</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Recent trauma, even minor, is a clue for arterial dissection in patients without typical neurologic findings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Headache History">
  <data key="d0">Headache History</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A history of the headache being different from prior episodes is a significant clue, reported by nearly all migraine patients describing dissection-related headaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Noncontrast Head CT">
  <data key="d0">Noncontrast Head CT</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Noncontrast head CT is an initial imaging step for evaluating headache in suspected arterial dissection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CT and CTA of the Head and Neck">
  <data key="d0">CT and CTA of the Head and Neck</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CT and CTA of the head and neck with IV contrast is recommended for diagnosing arterial dissection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MRI and MRA of the Head and Neck">
  <data key="d0">MRI and MRA of the Head and Neck</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">MRI and MRA of the head and neck without and with IV contrast is an equivalent diagnostic option to CT and CTA.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CTA or MRA of the Neck">
  <data key="d0">CTA or MRA of the Neck</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CTA or MRA of the neck evaluates the carotid and vertebral arteries from the thoracic inlet to the circle of Willis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sensitivity and Specificity Data">
  <data key="d0">Sensitivity and Specificity Data</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Reported sensitivity for CTA/MRA ranges from 50 to 80 percent and specificity from 67 to 99 percent for both CT and MRI modalities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Older Adult with New Headache">
  <data key="d0">Older Adult with New Headache</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A new or progressively worsening headache in a patient older than 50 years may suggest underlying tumor or hemorrhage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Immunosuppressed Patient with New Headache">
  <data key="d0">Immunosuppressed Patient with New Headache</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Infection, lymphoma, and leukemia are complications of chronic immunosuppression requiring MRI evaluation for abscess or encephalitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cancer Patient with New Headache">
  <data key="d0">Cancer Patient with New Headache</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Metastases and infection are primary concerns for cancer patients presenting with new headache, especially if immunocompromised.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Headache in Pregnancy">
  <data key="d0">New Headache in Pregnancy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Preeclampsia and eclampsia must be considered as causes of headache in pregnant women over 20 weeks gestation, along with arterial dissection and other less common causes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Orbital, Sinonasal, or Oromaxillofacial Headache">
  <data key="d0">Orbital, Sinonasal, or Oromaxillofacial Headache</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Diagnostic workup for headache with localizing features to the face in the emergency setting.&lt;SEP&gt;Headache with localizing features to the face in the emergency setting should be pursued in patients with high-risk features.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cervicocephalic Arterial Dissection">
  <data key="d0">Cervicocephalic Arterial Dissection</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Cervicocephalic arterial dissection is a medical condition involving tearing of the arterial wall in the neck and head region.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elevated ICP">
  <data key="d0">Elevated ICP</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Elevated intracranial pressure (ICP) is a clinical concern evaluated in various headache presentations, including tumor, hemorrhage, and mass effect.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptococcal Meningitis">
  <data key="d0">Cryptococcal Meningitis</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Cryptococcal meningitis is an infection that may be investigated via lumbar puncture if imaging is nondiagnostic in immunosuppressed patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Leptomeningeal Metastases">
  <data key="d0">Leptomeningeal Metastases</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Leptomeningeal metastases are a consideration in cancer patients with new headache, especially if immunocompromised.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RCVS">
  <data key="d0">RCVS</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">RCVS (Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction Syndrome) is a less common cause of headache in the pregnant or postpartum patient.&lt;SEP&gt;RCVS (Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction Syndrome) is a less common cause of headache in pregnancy and postpartum period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Head MRI without and with contrast">
  <data key="d0">Head MRI without and with contrast</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Head MRI without and with contrast is recommended for evaluating new headache in older adults, immunosuppressed patients, and cancer patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Head CT without contrast">
  <data key="d0">Head CT without contrast</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Head CT without contrast is an initial imaging procedure performed to evaluate patients with sudden-onset thunderclap headache.&lt;SEP&gt;Head CT without contrast is performed after antibiotics to look for contraindications to lumbar puncture.&lt;SEP&gt;Head CT without contrast is an option to evaluate for findings associated with elevated ICP when MRI is not immediately available.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CT with and without contrast">
  <data key="d0">CT with and without contrast</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CT with and without contrast is the second-line imaging option performed if MRI is contraindicated or not available.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lumbar Puncture">
  <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Lumbar puncture is performed to obtain cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) for analysis, especially if the head CT is negative for hemorrhage.&lt;SEP&gt;patient response to analgesics should not be used as a diagnostic tool and should not dissuade performance of lumbar puncture (LP) when indicated by history or examination.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbar puncture is performed to measure opening pressure and evaluate for underlying infection or leptomeningeal metastases if imaging is nondiagnostic.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbar puncture is a diagnostic procedure that may be required and necessitates withholding NSAIDs in certain patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Preeclampsia">
  <data key="d0">Preeclampsia</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Preeclampsia: Clinical features and diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Preeclampsia is a pregnancy-related condition causing headache and seizure, potentially presenting up to six weeks postpartum.&lt;SEP&gt;Preeclampsia presentation can overlap with PRES in pregnant patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Preeclampsia must be considered as a cause of headache in every pregnant woman over 20 weeks of gestation and can occur up to a few weeks postpartum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eclampsia">
  <data key="d0">Eclampsia</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Eclampsia must be considered as a cause of headache in every pregnant woman over 20 weeks of gestation and can occur up to a few weeks postpartum.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711753</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis">
  <data key="d0">Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">If the indication for the medication was a venous thrombotic event.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis is a particular concern in the postpartum period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Olanzapine">
  <data key="d0">Olanzapine</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Olanzapine is used to treat undifferentiated headache in the ED.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Injectable Opioids">
  <data key="d0">Injectable Opioids</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The use of injectable opioids is strongly discouraged but may be necessary for patients with contraindications to NSAIDs or vasoconstrictive effects, or for those unresponsive to prochlorperazine and diphenhydramine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ramirez-Lassepas M">
  <data key="d0">Ramirez-Lassepas M</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ramirez-Lassepas M is an author of a study on predictors of intracranial pathologic findings in headache patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Espinosa CE">
  <data key="d0">Espinosa CE</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Espinosa CE is an author of a study on predictors of intracranial pathologic findings in headache patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cicero JJ">
  <data key="d0">Cicero JJ</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Cicero JJ is an author of a study on predictors of intracranial pathologic findings in headache patients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Torelli P">
  <data key="d0">Torelli P</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Torelli P is an author of a literature review on management of primary headaches in adult Emergency Departments.&lt;SEP&gt;Torelli P is an author of a literature review on primary headache management in emergency departments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Campana V">
  <data key="d0">Campana V</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Campana V is an author of a literature review on management of primary headaches in adult Emergency Departments.&lt;SEP&gt;Campana V is an author of a literature review on primary headache management in emergency departments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cervellin G">
  <data key="d0">Cervellin G</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Cervellin G is an author of a literature review on management of primary headaches in adult Emergency Departments.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervellin G is an author of a literature review on primary headache management in emergency departments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711754</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Manzoni GC">
  <data key="d0">Manzoni GC</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Manzoni GC is an author of a literature review on management of primary headaches in adult Emergency Departments.&lt;SEP&gt;Manzoni GC is an author of a literature review on primary headache management in emergency departments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Camargo CA Jr">
  <data key="d0">Camargo CA Jr</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Camargo CA Jr is an author of a study on headache in United States emergency departments.&lt;SEP&gt;Camargo CA Jr is an author of a study on headache demographics and work-up in US emergency departments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pelletier AJ">
  <data key="d0">Pelletier AJ</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Pelletier AJ is an author of a study on headache in United States emergency departments.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelletier AJ is an author of a study on headache demographics and work-up in US emergency departments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Edlow JA">
  <data key="d0">Edlow JA</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Edlow JA is an author of a study on headache in United States emergency departments.&lt;SEP&gt;Edlow JA is an author of multiple studies on headache management and reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;Edlow JA is an author of a study on diagnosis of acute neurological emergencies in pregnant and post-partum women.&lt;SEP&gt;Edlow JA is an author of a narrative review on Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction Syndrome.&lt;SEP&gt;Edlow JA is an author of multiple studies on diagnostic accuracy in neurology and emergency medicine.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67064a19688d3727bfd01270acdcdada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CT or MRI">
  <data key="d0">CT or MRI</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">CT or MRI of the head is the preferred imaging examination for evaluating acute headache, with choice of modality depending on suspected diagnosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CTA or MRA">
  <data key="d0">CTA or MRA</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CTA or MRA is performed for imaging of the vessels, usually requiring intravenous contrast administration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PRES">
  <data key="d0">PRES</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">PRES (Reversible posterior leukoencephalopathy syndrome) is a neurological condition associated with hypertensive disorders and certain medications.&lt;SEP&gt;PRES can cause headache and seizure in the postpartum period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cervico-Cranial Artery Dissections">
  <data key="d0">Cervico-Cranial Artery Dissections</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Cervico-cranial artery dissections are vascular tears in the neck-to-head arteries that can cause gradual-onset headaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acute-Angle Closure Glaucoma">
  <data key="d0">Acute-Angle Closure Glaucoma</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Acute-angle closure glaucoma is an eye condition where pressure builds up suddenly, sometimes causing headaches that mimic migraine.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CNS Infection">
  <data key="d0">CNS Infection</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">CNS infection refers to central nervous system infections that can present with severe headaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Paranasal Sinuses">
  <data key="d0">Paranasal Sinuses</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Paranasal sinuses are air-filled spaces in the skull that can become infected and serve as a nidus for meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mastoid Sinuses">
  <data key="d0">Mastoid Sinuses</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Mastoid sinuses are part of the temporal bone that can serve as a nidus for intracranial abscess.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pharynx">
  <data key="d0">Pharynx</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Pharynx is the throat region that can serve as a nidus for meningitis or abscess.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inner Ear">
  <data key="d0">Inner Ear</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Inner ear structures can serve as a nidus for developing meningitis or intracranial abscess.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Altered Mental Status">
  <data key="d0">Altered Mental Status</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Altered mental status, personality changes, or fluctuating consciousness suggest potentially serious neurological abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;some patients with worrisome high-risk features (eg, altered mental status, a new hard neurologic deficit or associated seizure), should be admitted for observation and further consultation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Syncope">
  <data key="d0">Syncope</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Syncope or near-syncope at headache onset is suggestive of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hypoglycemia">
  <data key="d0">Hypoglycemia</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypoglycemia (low blood sugar) should be checked in patients with headache and altered mental status using a point-of-care (eg, fingerstick) glucose concentration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction">
  <data key="d0">Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction is a cause of sudden severe headache, often with exertion.&lt;SEP&gt;Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction can occur in the pregnancy and postpartum state.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Giant Cell Arteritis">
  <data key="d0">Giant Cell Arteritis</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Giant cell arteritis is an inflammatory condition of large arteries, posing greater risk for patients over 50 with new onset headaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Giant cell arteritis can be associated with headache and visual disturbances.&lt;SEP&gt;Giant cell arteritis is often, but not always, focused in a temple.&lt;SEP&gt;Giant cell arteritis should be suspected in patients with temporal artery abnormalities, particularly if associated with jaw claudication, sudden monocular vision loss, or unexplained fever or anemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Immunosuppression">
  <data key="d0">Immunosuppression</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Immunosuppression refers to a weakened immune system, increasing risk for intracranial disease.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Toxoplasmosis">
  <data key="d0">Toxoplasmosis</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Toxoplasmosis is an opportunistic infection in HIV/AIDS patients that can affect the CNS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CNS Malignancy">
  <data key="d0">CNS Malignancy</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">CNS malignancy refers to cancer of the central nervous system.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711755</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spontaneous Intracranial Hypotension">
  <data key="d0">Spontaneous Intracranial Hypotension</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Spontaneous intracranial hypotension (in patients without a spinal anesthetic) due to dural tears sustained during labor-related pushing.&lt;SEP&gt;Spontaneous intracranial hypotension: Pathophysiology, clinical features, and diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Spontaneous intracranial hypotension can occur due to dural tears during labor-related pushing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Location of Pain">
  <data key="d0">Location of Pain</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Location of the pain is a factor in headache assessment, though the text is cut off.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lyme disease">
  <data key="d0">Lyme disease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lyme disease is an infection that may present with headache as a prominent symptom in certain geographic regions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cerebral venous thrombosis">
  <data key="d0">Cerebral venous thrombosis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cerebral venous thrombosis is a condition related to coagulation issues and may present with headache.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerebral venous thrombosis: Etiology, clinical features, and diagnosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Postdural puncture headache">
  <data key="d0">Postdural puncture headache</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Postdural puncture headache is caused by low ICP from cerebrospinal fluid leaks following a lumbar puncture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Encephalitis">
  <data key="d0">Encephalitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Encephalitis is an infection that may present with headache and is suggested by altered mental status.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intracranial hemorrhage">
  <data key="d0">Intracranial hemorrhage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intracranial hemorrhage is a serious cause of headache associated with focal neurologic findings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acute angle-closure glaucoma">
  <data key="d0">Acute angle-closure glaucoma</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Acute angle-closure glaucoma is a serious cause of headache associated with focal neurologic findings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Carotid or vertebral artery dissection">
  <data key="d0">Carotid or vertebral artery dissection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Carotid or vertebral artery dissection is a serious cause of headache associated with focal neurologic findings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table 4">
  <data key="d0">Table 4</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 4 provides information on hypertensive retinopathy and encephalopathy in adults.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table 5">
  <data key="d0">Table 5</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 5 describes the concept of positive versus negative phenomena to distinguish migraine from ischemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CNS infection">
  <data key="d0">CNS infection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">CNS infection is a cause of fever that makes a diagnosis of migraine or tension-type headache unlikely.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CNS inflammation">
  <data key="d0">CNS inflammation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">CNS inflammation is a cause of fever that makes a diagnosis of migraine or tension-type headache unlikely.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SAH">
  <data key="d0">SAH</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Subarachnoid hemorrhage.&lt;SEP&gt;SAH (subarachnoid hemorrhage) is a cause of severe hypertension and can manifest as headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="hypertensive encephalopathy">
  <data key="d0">hypertensive encephalopathy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypertensive encephalopathy is associated with severe hypertension and can manifest as headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="liver disease">
  <data key="d0">liver disease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Liver disease adversely affects coagulation and may predispose patients to intracranial bleeding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="clotting disorders">
  <data key="d0">clotting disorders</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clotting disorders may predispose patients to intracranial bleeding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="hypercoagulable states">
  <data key="d0">hypercoagulable states</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypercoagulable states may increase the risk of stroke or cerebral venous thrombosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="point-of-care glucose concentration">
  <data key="d0">point-of-care glucose concentration</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Point-of-care glucose concentration (e.g., fingerstick) is used to check for hypoglycemia in patients with headache and altered mental status.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="fingerstick">
  <data key="d0">fingerstick</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fingerstick is a method used for point-of-care glucose concentration testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-44f11db3bcd7ce2dafa15cd85b96c021</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction Syndromes">
  <data key="d0">Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction Syndromes</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndromes (RCVS) are the second most common cause of thunderclap headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Computed Tomography with Angiography">
  <data key="d0">Computed Tomography with Angiography</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Computed tomography with angiography (CTA) is used to identify underlying causes such as cerebral aneurysm or arteriovenous malformation after a positive head CT or LP.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Magnetic Resonance Angiography">
  <data key="d0">Magnetic Resonance Angiography</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Magnetic resonance angiography (MRA) is used to identify underlying vascular causes of headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Catheter Angiography">
  <data key="d0">Catheter Angiography</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Catheter angiography is used for definitive vascular imaging when non-invasive angiography is inconclusive.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Quality Standards Subcommittee">
  <data key="d0">Quality Standards Subcommittee</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The Quality Standards Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology conducted research on imaging utilization in headache patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Quality Standards Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology reported on neurologic examination standards.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67064a19688d3727bfd01270acdcdada</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="National Hospital Ambulatory Medical Care Survey">
  <data key="d0">National Hospital Ambulatory Medical Care Survey</data>
  <data key="d1">datasource</data>
  <data key="d2">The National Hospital Ambulatory Medical Care Survey (NHAMCS) is a data source reporting on emergency department patient imaging and diagnoses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cerebral Aneurysm">
  <data key="d0">Cerebral Aneurysm</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Cerebral aneurysm is the most common underlying cause of subarachnoid hemorrhage identified through angiography.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Arteriovenous Malformation">
  <data key="d0">Arteriovenous Malformation</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Arteriovenous malformation is another common underlying cause of subarachnoid hemorrhage identified through angiography.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711756</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Neurology Consultation">
  <data key="d0">Neurology Consultation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Neurology consultation may facilitate both the acute evaluation and follow-up for patients with hard neurologic examination findings and nondiagnostic testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
