  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Direct Oral Anticoagulants">
  <data key="d0">Direct Oral Anticoagulants</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Patients on any oral anticoagulant plus antiplatelet agents have an increased risk compared with those not on these agents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sympathomimetics">
  <data key="d0">Sympathomimetics</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Sympathomimetics are also associated with intracranial bleeding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Analgesics">
  <data key="d0">Analgesics</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Analgesics can mask severe symptoms or sometimes exacerbate migraine headache through a rebound effect (medication overuse headache).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intracranial Bleeding">
  <data key="d0">Intracranial Bleeding</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Intracranial bleeding risk associated with anticoagulants or NSAIDs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Illicit Drugs">
  <data key="d0">Illicit Drugs</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A number of illicit drugs, including cocaine, methamphetamine, and other sympathomimetic agents, increase the risk of stroke and intracranial bleeding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Methamphetamine">
  <data key="d0">Methamphetamine</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Illicit drug that increases the risk of stroke and intracranial bleeding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Toxic Exposure">
  <data key="d0">Toxic Exposure</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Headaches that involve multiple family members or coworkers and improve rapidly in the emergency department (ED) without intervention, particularly during winter months, raise the possibility of carbon monoxide poisoning.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chiropractic Neck Manipulation">
  <data key="d0">Chiropractic Neck Manipulation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Chiropractic neck manipulation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Malignancy">
  <data key="d0">Malignancy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Malignancy with a risk of intracranial metastasis (and possibly increased intracranial pressure [ICP]).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polycystic Kidney Disease">
  <data key="d0">Polycystic Kidney Disease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Polycystic kidney disease or connective tissue disease, both of which increase the risk of aneurysms with resultant SAH.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711758</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Connective Tissue Disease">
  <data key="d0">Connective Tissue Disease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Polycystic kidney disease or connective tissue disease, both of which increase the risk of aneurysms with resultant SAH.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jaw Claudication">
  <data key="d0">Jaw Claudication</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Jaw claudication suggests giant cell arteritis as the cause of headache.&lt;SEP&gt;Giant cell arteritis should be suspected in patients with temporal artery abnormalities, particularly if associated with jaw claudication, sudden monocular vision loss, or unexplained fever or anemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Erythema Migrans">
  <data key="d0">Erythema Migrans</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Recent rash compatible with erythema migrans (picture 1) suggests Lyme meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Petechial Rash">
  <data key="d0">Petechial Rash</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent petechial (picture 2) or purpuric (picture 3) rash suggests bacterial meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Purpuric Rash">
  <data key="d0">Purpuric Rash</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent petechial (picture 2) or purpuric (picture 3) rash suggests bacterial meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Postural or Positional Headache">
  <data key="d0">Postural or Positional Headache</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Postural or positional headache raises concern for spontaneous intracranial hypotension or colloid cyst of the third ventricle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bacterial Meningitis">
  <data key="d0">Bacterial Meningitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent petechial (picture 2) or purpuric (picture 3) rash suggests bacterial meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lyme Disease">
  <data key="d0">Lyme Disease</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Recent rash compatible with erythema migrans (picture 1) suggests Lyme meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Social History">
  <data key="d0">Social History</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A social history, inclusive of hobbies and occupational exposures, may reveal potential causes of headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Occupational Exposures">
  <data key="d0">Occupational Exposures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A social history, inclusive of hobbies and occupational exposures, may reveal potential causes of headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hobbies">
  <data key="d0">Hobbies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A social history, inclusive of hobbies and occupational exposures, may reveal potential causes of headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bral Venous Sinus Thrombosis">
  <data key="d0">Bral Venous Sinus Thrombosis</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Bral venous sinus thrombosis is a particular concern in the postpartum period.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infection">
  <data key="d0">Infection</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Infection involving the orbits or cavernous sinus could indicate headache with visual impairment, periorbital pain, or ophthalmoplegia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inflammation">
  <data key="d0">Inflammation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Inflammation involving the orbits or cavernous sinus could indicate headache with visual impairment, periorbital pain, or ophthalmoplegia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tumor">
  <data key="d0">Tumor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tumor involving the orbits or cavernous sinus could indicate headache with visual impairment, periorbital pain, or ophthalmoplegia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MRI of the Head and Orbits">
  <data key="d0">MRI of the Head and Orbits</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">MRI of the head and orbits without and with contrast would be the preferred imaging examination for headache with visual impairment, periorbital pain, or ophthalmoplegia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Urgent Ophthalmic Examination">
  <data key="d0">Urgent Ophthalmic Examination</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">If acute angle-closure glaucoma is suspected, an urgent ophthalmic examination is indicated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nasal Endoscopy">
  <data key="d0">Nasal Endoscopy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Headache of sinonasal origin is best evaluated with nasal endoscopy performed by an ENT specialist as an outpatient.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Head MRI without and with Contrast">
  <data key="d0">Head MRI without and with Contrast</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">If intracranial complications of sinus disease are suspected, head MRI without and with contrast is indicated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CT">
  <data key="d0">CT</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Headache suspected of odontogenic origin is better evaluated with CT, especially in the acute setting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Temporal Artery Abnormalities">
  <data key="d0">Temporal Artery Abnormalities</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Giant cell arteritis should be suspected in patients with temporal artery abnormalities (eg, tenderness, decreased pulsations).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Disposition">
  <data key="d0">Disposition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Patients with high-risk features in whom a secondary cause of headache is discovered are admitted or referred to the appropriate setting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High-Risk Features">
  <data key="d0">High-Risk Features</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Patients with high-risk features in whom a secondary cause of headache is discovered are admitted or referred to the appropriate setting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Hard Neurologic Deficit">
  <data key="d0">New Hard Neurologic Deficit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">some patients with worrisome high-risk features (eg, altered mental status, a new hard neurologic deficit or associated seizure), should be admitted for observation and further consultation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Associated Seizure">
  <data key="d0">Associated Seizure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">some patients with worrisome high-risk features (eg, altered mental status, a new hard neurologic deficit or associated seizure), should be admitted for observation and further consultation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Symptomatic Treatment">
  <data key="d0">Symptomatic Treatment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">For many patients in whom a secondary cause was suspected but a thorough, appropriate workup was normal, symptomatic treatment and discharge with timely primary care or neurologic follow-up is reasonable.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Relief of Symptoms">
  <data key="d0">Relief of Symptoms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Relief of symptoms is an important part of management for patients presenting to the emergency department with severe headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Symptomatic Treatment for Undifferentiated Headache">
  <data key="d0">Symptomatic Treatment for Undifferentiated Headache</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">For those patients with a primary headache disorder that does not clearly meet criteria for migraine or cluster headache, symptomatic treatment should be provided.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parenterally Administered NSAID">
  <data key="d0">Parenterally Administered NSAID</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The authors of a systematic review of headache management in the ED propose the use of a parenterally administered nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) and a dopamine antagonist.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dopamine Antagonist">
  <data key="d0">Dopamine Antagonist</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The authors of a systematic review of headache management in the ED propose the use of a parenterally administered nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) and a dopamine antagonist.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711759</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Benztropine">
  <data key="d0">Benztropine</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Pretreatment with 12.5 mg of diphenhydramine or 1 mg of benztropine is suggested to avoid akathisia or other extrapyramidal symptoms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Low-Dose Haloperidol">
  <data key="d0">Low-Dose Haloperidol</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Two small randomized trials report that low-dose haloperidol is effective for alleviating severe pain due to a benign cause of headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Visual Impairment">
  <data key="d0">Visual Impairment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Headache with visual impairment could indicate acute angle-closure glaucoma, infection, inflammation, or tumor involving the orbits or cavernous sinus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Periorbital Pain">
  <data key="d0">Periorbital Pain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Headache with periorbital pain could indicate acute angle-closure glaucoma, infection, inflammation, or tumor involving the orbits or cavernous sinus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ophthalmoplegia">
  <data key="d0">Ophthalmoplegia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Headache with ophthalmoplegia could indicate acute angle-closure glaucoma, infection, inflammation, or tumor involving the orbits or cavernous sinus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Orbital Cellulitis">
  <data key="d0">Orbital Cellulitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Headache with visual impairment could indicate acute angle-closure glaucoma, infection, inflammation, or tumor involving the orbits or cavernous sinus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tolosa-Hunt Syndrome">
  <data key="d0">Tolosa-Hunt Syndrome</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Headache with visual impairment could indicate acute angle-closure glaucoma, infection, inflammation, or tumor involving the orbits or cavernous sinus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dental Amalgam">
  <data key="d0">Dental Amalgam</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Dental amalgam degrades image quality with both CT and MRI, and in such cases, imaging is likely to be of limited quality.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Temporomandibular Joint Disorders">
  <data key="d0">Temporomandibular Joint Disorders</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Headache suspected to originate from maxillofacial conditions such as temporomandibular joint (TMJ) disorders or trigeminal neuralgia is best evaluated with head MRI without and with IV contrast.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Odontogenic Origin">
  <data key="d0">Odontogenic Origin</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Headache suspected of odontogenic origin is better evaluated with CT, especially in the acute setting.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sudden Monocular Vision Loss">
  <data key="d0">Sudden Monocular Vision Loss</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Giant cell arteritis should be suspected in patients with temporal artery abnormalities, particularly if associated with jaw claudication, sudden monocular vision loss, or unexplained fever or anemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unexplained Fever">
  <data key="d0">Unexplained Fever</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Giant cell arteritis should be suspected in patients with temporal artery abnormalities, particularly if associated with jaw claudication, sudden monocular vision loss, or unexplained fever or anemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unexplained Anemia">
  <data key="d0">Unexplained Anemia</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Giant cell arteritis should be suspected in patients with temporal artery abnormalities, particularly if associated with jaw claudication, sudden monocular vision loss, or unexplained fever or anemia.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-36b9bf57cb93bcd7302da2d7f4b4041b</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Traumatic lumbar puncture">
  <data key="d0">Traumatic lumbar puncture</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Traumatic lumbar puncture is a clinical dilemma in the ED to determine appropriate workup and disposition for patients at risk for SAH with unrevealing head CT and initial traumatic LP.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Suspected meningitis or encephalitis">
  <data key="d0">Suspected meningitis or encephalitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Suspected meningitis or encephalitis is indicated by fever and altered mental status, with or without nuchal rigidity, suggesting central nervous system infection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Opening pressure">
  <data key="d0">Opening pressure</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Elevated opening pressure (greater than 20 cm of water) suggests a pathologic process in the context of traumatic lumbar puncture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cerebrovascular imaging">
  <data key="d0">Cerebrovascular imaging</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Cerebrovascular imaging, including head MRI with contrast and craniocervical MRA or head CT with contrast and craniocervical CTA, is recommended to resolve diagnostic ambiguity in suspected SAH.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lumbar puncture">
  <data key="d0">Lumbar puncture</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Lumbar puncture is a procedure to obtain cerebrospinal fluid for analysis, with specific guidelines for performance in the ED.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Immunocompromised state">
  <data key="d0">Immunocompromised state</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Immunocompromised state (e.g., HIV infection, immunosuppressive therapy) is a criterion for prior CT before lumbar puncture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active CNS disease">
  <data key="d0">Active CNS disease</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">Active CNS disease (e.g., acute stroke, mass lesion, abscess) causing intracranial mass effect is a criterion for prior CT before lumbar puncture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New-onset seizure">
  <data key="d0">New-onset seizure</data>
  <data key="d1">condition</data>
  <data key="d2">New-onset seizure within one week of presentation is a criterion for prior CT before lumbar puncture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abnormal level of consciousness">
  <data key="d0">Abnormal level of consciousness</data>
  <data key="d1">sign</data>
  <data key="d2">Abnormal level of consciousness is a criterion for prior CT before lumbar puncture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Focal neurologic deficit">
  <data key="d0">Focal neurologic deficit</data>
  <data key="d1">sign</data>
  <data key="d2">Focal neurologic deficit is a criterion for prior CT before lumbar puncture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blood cultures">
  <data key="d0">Blood cultures</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Blood cultures should be obtained if bacterial meningitis is considered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Empiric antimicrobial therapy">
  <data key="d0">Empiric antimicrobial therapy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Empiric antimicrobial therapy (with or without IV dexamethasone) should be instituted if bacterial meningitis is considered.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IV acyclovir">
  <data key="d0">IV acyclovir</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Empiric treatment with IV acyclovir for herpes encephalitis should be given if herpes encephalitis is a serious consideration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711760</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IV doxycycline">
  <data key="d0">IV doxycycline</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Empiric treatment with IV doxycycline for tick-borne diseases (e.g., Rocky Mountain spotted fever, anaplasmosis) should be given if these entities are serious considerations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fungal meningitis">
  <data key="d0">Fungal meningitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Fungal meningitis (e.g., cryptococcal) should be considered in the immunocompromised host or resource-limited settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tuberculous meningitis">
  <data key="d0">Tuberculous meningitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tuberculous meningitis should be considered in the immunocompromised host or resource-limited settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intracranial mass effect">
  <data key="d0">Intracranial mass effect</data>
  <data key="d1">sign</data>
  <data key="d2">Intracranial mass effect caused by active CNS disease is a contraindication to lumbar puncture without imaging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Herniation">
  <data key="d0">Herniation</data>
  <data key="d1">complication</data>
  <data key="d2">Rare documented cases of herniation following lumbar puncture have made some clinicians reluctant to perform the procedure without prior imaging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Table 6">
  <data key="d0">Table 6</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 6 is referenced in the context of empiric antimicrobial therapy for bacterial meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Herpes encephalitis">
  <data key="d0">Herpes encephalitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Herpes encephalitis is a serious consideration requiring empiric treatment with IV acyclovir.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tick-borne diseases">
  <data key="d0">Tick-borne diseases</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tick-borne diseases (e.g., Rocky Mountain spotted fever, anaplasmosis) are serious considerations requiring empiric treatment with IV doxycycline.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cryptococcal meningitis">
  <data key="d0">Cryptococcal meningitis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cryptococcal meningitis is a fungal infection to consider in the immunocompromised host or resource-limited settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Central nervous system tuberculosis">
  <data key="d0">Central nervous system tuberculosis</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Central nervous system tuberculosis is a consideration for meningitis in the immunocompromised host or resource-limited settings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intensive care unit">
  <data key="d0">Intensive care unit</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Intensive care unit is the recommended admission location for patients with lumbar puncture contraindications based on CT findings.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Neurologist">
  <data key="d0">Neurologist</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Neurologist is a physician to be consulted for diagnostic uncertainty or lumbar puncture contraindications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Infectious disease physician">
  <data key="d0">Infectious disease physician</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Infectious disease physician is to be consulted for patients with lumbar puncture contraindications or suspected infection.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thompson C">
  <data key="d0">Thompson C</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Thompson C is an author of a study on clinical features in patients with nontraumatic headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rylance J">
  <data key="d0">Rylance J</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Rylance J is an author of a study on clinical features in patients with nontraumatic headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mason SM">
  <data key="d0">Mason SM</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mason SM is an author of a study on clinical features in patients with nontraumatic headache.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ward TN">
  <data key="d0">Ward TN</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ward TN is an author of a study on evaluation and management of headache in the emergency department.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Levin M">
  <data key="d0">Levin M</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Levin M is an author of a study on evaluation and management of headache in the emergency department.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phillips JM">
  <data key="d0">Phillips JM</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Phillips JM is an author of a study on evaluation and management of headache in the emergency department.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
  <data key="d4">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d5">1792711761</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Perry JJ">
  <data key="d0">Perry JJ</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Perry JJ is an author of a study on the Ottawa Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Rule.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e0b3b281b34d994290b07d55d736c0fd</data>
