<edge source="Thunderclap Headache" target="Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction Syndromes">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndromes are the second most common cause of thunderclap headache.</data>
  <data key="d9">cause classification,headache subtype</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711768</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thunderclap Headache" target="Head CT without contrast">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A head CT without contrast should be performed as the initial imaging procedure for sudden-onset thunderclap headache.</data>
  <data key="d9">initial imaging,sudden-onset headache evaluation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711769</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Thunderclap Headache" target="Colloid Cyst of the Third Ventricle">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Overview of thunderclap headache, section on 'Colloid cyst of the third ventricle'.</data>
  <data key="d9">anatomical association,headache type</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711772</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction Syndrome" target="Sudden-Onset Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Sudden-onset headache is a key feature of reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome, often with identified triggers.</data>
  <data key="d9">headache type,serious condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7d7e2a1fa6fae352f798fe0d6e8e0e62</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Carotid and Vertebral Artery Dissections" target="Sudden-Onset Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Sudden-onset headache may be associated with carotid and vertebral artery dissections, though these often present gradually.</data>
  <data key="d9">headache type,serious condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7d7e2a1fa6fae352f798fe0d6e8e0e62</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Venous Sinus Thrombosis" target="Sudden-Onset Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Sudden-onset headache may be associated with venous sinus thrombosis, though these often present gradually.</data>
  <data key="d9">headache type,serious condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7d7e2a1fa6fae352f798fe0d6e8e0e62</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Venous Sinus Thrombosis" target="Cervico-Cranial Artery Dissections">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Both cervico-cranial artery dissections and venous sinus thrombosis can present with gradual-onset headaches.</data>
  <data key="d9">headache etiology,vascular pathology</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Venous Sinus Thrombosis" target="Postpartum Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Venous sinus thrombosis is most common postpartum.</data>
  <data key="d9">headache etiology,postpartum frequency</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Venous Sinus Thrombosis" target="Postpartum Seizure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Venous sinus thrombosis is most common postpartum.</data>
  <data key="d9">hypertensive disorder,postpartum complication</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711767</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pituitary Apoplexy" target="Sudden-Onset Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Sudden-onset headache can indicate pituitary apoplexy.</data>
  <data key="d9">headache type,serious condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7d7e2a1fa6fae352f798fe0d6e8e0e62</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pituitary Apoplexy" target="New Headache in Pregnancy">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Pituitary apoplexy is a less common cause of headache in pregnancy and postpartum period.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical association,differential diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Pituitary Apoplexy" target="Pregnancy Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Less common etiologies include pituitary apoplexy.</data>
  <data key="d9">pituitary tumor,pregnancy etiology</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711767</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma" target="Sudden-Onset Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Sudden-onset headache can indicate acute angle-closure glaucoma.</data>
  <data key="d9">headache type,serious condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-7d7e2a1fa6fae352f798fe0d6e8e0e62</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma" target="Intraocular Pressure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Elevated intraocular pressure confirms the diagnosis of acute angle-closure glaucoma.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical diagnosis,diagnostic confirmation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma" target="Visual Disturbances">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Patients with significant ophthalmologic disease, most notably acute angle-closure glaucoma, present with a complaint of headache; A careful history and physical examination, including measurement of intraocular pressures, is usually sufficient to determine whether this is the source of pain.</data>
  <data key="d9">headache association,ocular symptoms</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma" target="Involved Eye">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Headache from acute angle-closure glaucoma is commonly centered around the involved eye.</data>
  <data key="d9">secondary headache,symptom localization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711770</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Colloid Cyst of the Third Ventricle" target="Postural or Positional Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Postural or positional headache raises concern for spontaneous intracranial hypotension or colloid cyst of the third ventricle.</data>
  <data key="d9">anatomy,diagnostic concern</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711772</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Colloid Cyst of the Third Ventricle" target="Spontaneous Intracranial Hypotension">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Overview of thunderclap headache, section on 'Colloid cyst of the third ventricle'.</data>
  <data key="d9">anatomy,diagnostic concern</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711772</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lyme Meningitis" target="Erythema Migrans">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Recent rash compatible with erythema migrans (picture 1) suggests Lyme meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic indicator,infection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711771</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lyme Meningitis" target="Lyme Disease">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Recent rash compatible with erythema migrans (picture 1) suggests Lyme meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic indicator,infection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711771</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Brain Abscess" target="Intravenous Drug Use">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If the drugs are used intravenously (IV), brain abscess is another possibility, even in the absence of fever.</data>
  <data key="d9">complication,infection risk</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711771</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Posterior Reversible Encephalopathy Syndrome" target="New Headache in Pregnancy">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome is a less common cause of headache in pregnancy and postpartum period.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical association,differential diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension" target="Lumbar Puncture">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">In idiopathic intracranial hypertension, lumbar puncture with opening pressure is a necessary part of the evaluation but should follow brain imaging.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic procedure,pressure measurement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Idiopathic Intracranial Hypertension" target="PRES">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">PRES and idiopathic intracranial hypertension are conditions that can present with similar headache and visual symptoms.</data>
  <data key="d9">increased intracranial pressure,reversible posterior leukoencephalopathy syndrome</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Acute Bacterial Meningitis" target="Concurrent Rash Indicators">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Concurrent petechial (picture 2) or purpuric (picture 3) rash suggests bacterial meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic indicators,infection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711772</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Intraocular hemorrhage" target="Visual changes or loss">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Intraocular hemorrhage can result in vascular compromise leading to Visual changes or loss.</data>
  <data key="d9">retinal involvement,vascular compromise</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Intraocular hemorrhage" target="Pupillary asymmetry">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Intraocular hemorrhage may compress cranial nerves causing Pupillary asymmetry.</data>
  <data key="d9">cranial nerve compression</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Intraocular hemorrhage" target="Field cuts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Intraocular hemorrhage may involve the optic nerve leading to Field cuts.</data>
  <data key="d9">optic nerve involvement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Visual changes or loss" target="Pupillary asymmetry">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Visual changes or loss may be associated with cranial nerve dysfunction causing Pupillary asymmetry.</data>
  <data key="d9">cranial nerve involvement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Visual changes or loss" target="Field cuts">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Visual changes or loss can result from processes involving the optic nerve, chiasm, optic radiations, or occipital cortex causing Field cuts.</data>
  <data key="d9">chiasm,cortex involvement,optic nerve,radiations</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Field cuts" target="Pupillary asymmetry">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Both Field cuts and Pupillary asymmetry can help localize anatomical pathology such as carotid dissection or cranial nerve compression.</data>
  <data key="d9">anatomical localization</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Abnormalities of the temporal artery" target="Carotid bruit">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Abnormalities of the temporal artery may be associated with carotid artery dissection, which can cause a Carotid bruit.</data>
  <data key="d9">atherosclerotic plaque,giant cell arteritis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Abnormalities of the temporal artery" target="Nasal discharge">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Abnormalities of the temporal artery may reflect systemic involvement including Nasal discharge.</data>
  <data key="d9">giant cell arteritis,sinus involvement</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="CHOICE OF IMAGING EXAM" target="CT or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the head">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">CT or MRI is the preferred examination for headache with high-risk features.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic performance,imaging method</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="CHOICE OF IMAGING EXAM" target="CTA or MRA examinations">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">CTA or MRA is performed as an added examination usually requiring IV contrast for vessel imaging.</data>
  <data key="d9">IV contrast requirement,angiography method</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62e03c9a26baa74eae064a1bded7c588</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="American College of Emergency Physicians" target="Lumbar Puncture">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The American College of Emergency Physicians recommends that lumbar puncture without prior imaging may be performed in patients without signs of increased ICP.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical recommendation,imaging guidance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Horner Syndrome" target="Carotid Artery Dissection">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A significant percentage of patients with carotid and vertebral artery dissection will manifest Horner syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d9">associated neurological findings,vascular condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Horner Syndrome" target="Vertebral Artery Dissection">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Up to 19 percent of patients with vertebral artery dissection manifest Horner syndrome, unlike carotid dissection where 38 percent do.</data>
  <data key="d9">associated neurological findings,vascular condition</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Horner Syndrome" target="Arterial Dissection">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Horner syndrome is a clinical clue that may indicate arterial dissection in the absence of thunderclap onset.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical clue,diagnostic indicator</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Intracranial Mass Lesion" target="Increased Intracranial Pressure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Intracranial mass lesions are evaluated as a cause of increased intracranial pressure using head MRI or CT.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic evaluation,pathophysiology</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711763</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head CT" target="Increased Intracranial Pressure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Head CT is used to evaluate for findings associated with elevated ICP prior to lumbar puncture.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic tool,imaging modality</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head CT" target="Lumbar Puncture">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Head CT is an option to evaluate for findings associated with elevated ICP prior to lumbar puncture, and MRI is performed when available.</data>
  <data key="d9">imaging guidance,prior procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head MRI" target="Increased Intracranial Pressure">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Head MRI is used to evaluate for intracranial mass lesions and cerebral edema associated with increased ICP.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic tool,imaging modality</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head MRI" target="Lumbar Puncture">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">MRI without and with contrast is performed when the modality becomes available after initial CT, and should follow brain imaging prior to lumbar puncture in suspected idiopathic intracranial hypertension.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic sequence,imaging guidance</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Carboxyhemoglobin Concentration" target="Suspected carbon monoxide poisoning">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The diagnosis of carbon monoxide poisoning is confirmed by measuring carboxyhemoglobin concentration with cooximetry using an arterial blood gas sample.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic confirmation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Intraocular Pressure" target="Suspected acute angle-closure glaucoma">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Elevated intraocular pressure confirms the diagnosis, and unnecessary neurologic workup and imaging should be avoided to not delay treatment.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical urgency,diagnostic confirmation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-62c387dc1abd6c2085428bd5bab9c2b4</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Arterial Dissection" target="Brain Ischemia or Infarction">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Brain ischemia or infarction is a common presentation in patients with arterial dissection, occurring in nearly 60 percent of cases.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical presentation,symptom association</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Arterial Dissection" target="Recent Trauma">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Recent trauma, even minor, is a clue for arterial dissection in patients without typical neurologic findings.</data>
  <data key="d9">etiology,historical clue</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Arterial Dissection" target="Headache History">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">A history of the headache being different from prior episodes is a significant clue, reported by nearly all migraine patients describing dissection-related headaches.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic aid,historical clue</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Arterial Dissection" target="CTA or MRA of the Neck">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">CTA or MRA of the neck evaluates the carotid and vertebral arteries from the thoracic inlet to the circle of Willis in the context of arterial dissection.</data>
  <data key="d9">anatomical evaluation,imaging scope</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Arterial Dissection" target="New Headache in Pregnancy">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Arterial dissection is considered as a less common cause of headache in pregnant or postpartum patients.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical presentation,differential diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Arterial Dissection" target="Cervicocephalic Arterial Dissection">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Cervicocephalic arterial dissection is the specific anatomical term for arterial dissection presenting in the neck and head region.</data>
  <data key="d9">anatomical location,condition specification</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Arterial Dissection" target="Elevated ICP">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Elevated ICP is a concern evaluated in the context of arterial dissection and other headache etiologies.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical presentation,complication association</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Noncontrast Head CT" target="CT and CTA of the Head and Neck">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Noncontrast head CT should be followed by CT and CTA of the head and neck with IV contrast for diagnosing arterial dissection.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic pathway,imaging protocol</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Noncontrast Head CT" target="MRI and MRA of the Head and Neck">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">MRI and MRA of the head and neck without and with IV contrast is an equivalent option to CT and CTA for diagnosing arterial dissection.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic alternative,imaging protocol</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Older Adult with New Headache" target="Immunosuppressed Patient with New Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Both older adult and immunosuppressed patient presentations suggest underlying serious conditions (tumor/hemorrhage or infection/lymphoma/leukemia) requiring specific imaging approaches.</data>
  <data key="d9">patient demographics,underlying risk</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Older Adult with New Headache" target="Cancer Patient with New Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Both older adult and cancer patient presentations suggest underlying serious conditions (tumor or metastasis/infection) requiring specific imaging approaches.</data>
  <data key="d9">patient demographics,underlying risk</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Older Adult with New Headache" target="Elevated ICP">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Elevated ICP is evaluated in older adults with new headache to assess for tumor, hemorrhage, and mass effect.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical feature,diagnostic target</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Immunosuppressed Patient with New Headache" target="Cryptococcal Meningitis">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If imaging is nondiagnostic, lumbar puncture may be needed to investigate cryptococcal meningitis in immunosuppressed patients.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic pathway,infection complication</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Cancer Patient with New Headache" target="Leptomeningeal Metastases">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Leptomeningeal metastases are another consideration in cancer patients with new headache.</data>
  <data key="d9">complication consideration,diagnostic target</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="New Headache in Pregnancy" target="Orbital, Sinonasal, or Oromaxillofacial Headache">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">New headache in pregnancy must consider preeclampsia and eclampsia alongside other causes including orbital, sinonasal, or oromaxillofacial headache with localizing features.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical presentation,diagnostic category</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711764</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="New Headache in Pregnancy" target="Preeclampsia">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Preeclampsia must be considered as the cause of headache in every pregnant woman over 20 weeks of gestation.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical association,differential diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="New Headache in Pregnancy" target="Eclampsia">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Eclampsia must be considered as a cause of headache in every pregnant woman over 20 weeks of gestation and postpartum period.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical association,differential diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="New Headache in Pregnancy" target="Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis is a particular concern in the postpartum period.</data>
  <data key="d9">differential diagnosis,postpartum concern</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="New Headache in Pregnancy" target="RCVS">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">RCVS (Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction Syndrome) is a less common cause of headache in pregnancy and postpartum period.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical association,differential diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head MRI without and with contrast" target="Head CT without contrast">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Head CT without contrast is an option to evaluate for findings associated with elevated ICP when MRI is not immediately available, with MRI performed when the modality becomes available.</data>
  <data key="d9">imaging alternative,modality selection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head MRI without and with contrast" target="CT with and without contrast">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">CT with and without contrast is the second-line option and only performed if MRI is contraindicated or not available at all.</data>
  <data key="d9">imaging alternative,modality selection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head CT without contrast" target="Lumbar Puncture">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If the head CT is negative, a lumbar puncture should follow to evaluate for hemorrhage in patients with suspected subarachnoid headache.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic sequence,hemorrhage detection</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d93c3ae2e3eb96980253ed880c4042a8</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711768</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head CT without contrast" target="Suspected meningitis or encephalitis">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Head CT without contrast is performed after antibiotics to look for contraindications to LP.</data>
  <data key="d9">contraindication check,safety evaluation</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711772</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Head CT without contrast" target="After antibiotics">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">After antibiotics have been given, perform a head CT without contrast to look for contraindications to LP.</data>
  <data key="d9">contraindication check,safety protocol</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-d3b99ec17053435d72311359e33c6696</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711773</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lumbar Puncture" target="Imaging Nondiagnostic">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">If imaging is nondiagnostic, lumbar puncture may be needed to investigate other potential causes, including cryptococcal meningitis.</data>
  <data key="d9">diagnostic step,investigative procedure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-4b923e6b3f5a46729e0154937dbba3c5</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Preeclampsia" target="PRES">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Preeclampsia is the most common pregnancy-related diagnosis; the presentation of preeclampsia can overlap with PRES.</data>
  <data key="d9">hypertensive disorder,pregnancy complication</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-88449f41c18acfddbc679b10a20feb42</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Preeclampsia" target="Headache during pregnancy and postpartum">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Headache during pregnancy and postpartum.</data>
  <data key="d9">clinical features,diagnosis</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-ccbcc20d04c05bb1a254eb147d0d3a3f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711770</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Injectable Opioids" target="Contraindications to NSAIDs">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The use of injectable opioids is strongly discouraged, but they may be necessary for patients with contraindications to NSAIDs or medications with vasoconstrictive effects.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative therapy,clinical guideline</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711765</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Injectable Opioids" target="Prochlorperazine and Diphenhydramine">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Injectable opioids may be necessary for patients in whom prochlorperazine and diphenhydramine have not been effective.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative therapy,treatment failure</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Injectable Opioids" target="Contraindications">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">The use of injectable opioids is strongly discouraged but may be necessary for patients with contraindications to NSAIDs or vasoconstrictive effects.</data>
  <data key="d9">alternative therapy,clinical guideline</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-764b1321ae6bc951a2c8d2de5901567f</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711766</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Edlow JA" target="Dubosh NM">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Dubosh NM and Edlow JA co-authored a systematic review on the sensitivity of early brain computed tomography to exclude aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d9">collaboration,study authorship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67064a19688d3727bfd01270acdcdada</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711769</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Edlow JA" target="Shah KH">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Shah KH and Edlow JA co-authored a study on the incidence of traumatic lumbar puncture.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d9">collaboration,study authorship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67064a19688d3727bfd01270acdcdada</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711769</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Edlow JA" target="Gopal AK">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Gopal AK and Edlow JA co-authored a clinical evaluation on cranial computed tomography before lumbar puncture.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d9">collaboration,study authorship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67064a19688d3727bfd01270acdcdada</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711769</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Edlow JA" target="Silbert PL">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Silbert PL and Edlow JA co-authored a study on headache and neck pain in artery dissections.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d9">collaboration,study authorship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67064a19688d3727bfd01270acdcdada</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711769</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Edlow JA" target="Lyrer PA">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Lyrer PA and Edlow JA co-authored a study on the clinical import of Horner syndrome in artery dissection.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d9">collaboration,study authorship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67064a19688d3727bfd01270acdcdada</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711769</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Edlow JA" target="von Babo M">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">von Babo M and Edlow JA co-authored a study on differences and similarities between spontaneous artery dissections.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d9">collaboration,study authorship</data>
  <data key="d10">chunk-67064a19688d3727bfd01270acdcdada</data>
  <data key="d11">iMD_Uptodate_Evaluation_of_the_adult_with_nontraumatic_headache.PDF</data>
  <data key="d12">1792711769</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Edlow JA" target="Arnold M">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Arnold M and Edlow JA co-authored a study on pain as the only symptom of cervical artery dissection.&gt;</data>
  <data key="d9">collaboration,study authorship</data>
